<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Resources page</title>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag 'resources' %>
    <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
    <% content_for :title, "Resources page" %>
  </head>

  <body>
  <%= render "layouts/header" %>

  <div class="resources">
    <div class="column skinny">
    <br>

        <h1>MicroNet: Background</h1>
<br>
        <p>
          MicroNet is built on the work of Peter Tonner and Firas Midani. In particular, it uses the
          AMIGA pipeline developed by Firas, and partially based on work by Peter, that provides several frameworks for microbial
          growth analysis in Python. Please check out the in-depth vignette Firas has developed for AMIGA here:
          <a href="https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/">https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/</a>.

          MicroNet simplifies the AMIGA pipeline into a user-friendly webapp that doesn't require
          command-line knowledge. It is intended for researchers who would like to visualize their growth curves and/or perform basic analyses without resorting to command-line tools.
        </p>

        <h2>Demo Video</h2>

        <div class="resources-video-container">
          <video width="100%" controls>
            <source src="/docs/MicroNet Demo.mp4" type="video/mp4">
            Your browser does not support the video tag.
          </video>
        </div>

        <h1>Specific Analyses</h1>

        <h2>Raw Data (Per Well)</h2>

        <p>
          This analysis will generate a simple growth curve for each well in the user's dataset.
          According to the
          <a href="https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/doc/plotting.html">AMIGA vignette</a>,
          this analysis computes 'the fold-change for all wells relative to the negative control well.
          Fold-change is computed as maximum change in OD in each well relative to the first time point divided by the maximum change in OD in the control well relative to the first time point.
          The numbers on the top right of each plot is the raw maximum OD in each curve [...] wells where the fold-change is higher than 1.5 [are highlighted] in blue [while wells where the fold change is] lower than 0.5 [are highlighted] in red.'
        </p>

        <p>
          In MicroNet, the Raw Data Plot requires each uploaded data file to contain exactly 96 wells
          (a standard microplate). Files with a different number of wells will be rejected for this analysis.
        </p>

        <h2 id="test-differential-growth">Comparing Growth in Different Conditions</h2>

        <p>
          This analysis uses a Gaussian Process regression to test for differential growth between conditions.
          To do so, it uses the "test" function of the AMIGA pipeline. See
          <a href="https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/doc/hypothesistesting.html">
            https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/doc/hypothesistesting.html
          </a>
          for more about the command-line usage of this function.

          This analysis uses AMIGA's default number of permutations (99). The Gaussian Process
          time step size is now adjustable directly in MicroNet: when you set up your analyses you can
          choose a value from 1 (the default) up to 15. A larger time step size fits the model on fewer
          time points, which lowers memory usage on large datasets at the cost of some temporal
          resolution; MicroNet automatically caps your choice so the model always keeps at least 8 time
          points, and it will retry an analysis with progressively larger time step sizes if it runs out
          of memory (see <a href="#time-step-size">Time Step Size and Memory</a> below).

          The analysis will generate a normalized OD plot modelling the growth of the two conditions you selected.
          The thin lines surrounding the model curve indicate 95% confidence intervals.
          The output report will provide the log Bayes Factor value,
          as well as the confidence interval outside which this value must fall in order to indicate differential growth at an FDR of 10%.
          If you require additional help understanding the output of this analysis, please
          <a href="/contact">contact us</a>
          and someone on our team will be happy to assist you with interpretation via email.
        </p>

        <h2 id="gaussian-process-plot">Gaussian Process Plot</h2>

        <p>
          This analysis fits a Gaussian Process regression to your growth curves and plots the resulting
          model of normalized OD over time. The thin lines surrounding each curve indicate 95% confidence
          intervals. It is a good way to visualize the underlying growth trend after smoothing out
          measurement noise. You can optionally pool replicates by a metadata column (so that replicates of
          the same condition are modelled together) and/or restrict the plot to a subset of conditions.
        </p>

        <h2 id="fit-statistics">Fit Statistics</h2>

        <p>
          This analysis uses the "fit" function of the AMIGA pipeline to extract quantitative growth
          parameters from the Gaussian Process fit of each sample. MicroNet reports the parameters most
          commonly used to summarize growth: the area under the curve (AUC, in units of OD), the growth
          rate, the linear carrying capacity (in units of OD), the lag time, and the doubling time. If you
          choose to pool replicates by a metadata column, the statistics are reported per pooled condition
          rather than per individual well.
        </p>

        <h2 id="heatmap">Heatmap</h2>

        <p>
          This analysis visualizes a single growth parameter across two metadata dimensions as a
          color-coded grid. You choose the value to display (for example, growth rate) and the columns to
          use for the x- and y-axes; MicroNet runs an AMIGA fit and then renders the heatmap. It is
          especially useful for spotting patterns across a plate layout or across combinations of
          conditions. A mapping file is required for this analysis.
        </p>

        <h2 id="compare-parameters">Compare Parameters</h2>

        <p>
          This analysis uses the "compare" function of the AMIGA pipeline to statistically compare growth
          parameters between pooled conditions. It fits the data while sampling from the posterior
          distribution, which lets it report not just point estimates of each growth parameter but also a
          measure of the uncertainty around the difference between conditions. Use this when you want a
          quantitative comparison of parameters (rather than the visual comparison provided by the
          Gaussian Process Plot).
        </p>

        <h2 id="time-step-size">Time Step Size and Memory</h2>

        <p>
          Every analysis that fits a Gaussian Process model (the Gaussian Process Plot, Fit Statistics,
          Heatmap, Compare Parameters, and the differential-growth test above) uses a <em>time step
          size</em> that you can set when configuring your analyses. The default is 1, and you may choose
          any value up to 15. A larger time step size fits the model on fewer time points, which reduces
          the amount of memory the analysis needs on large datasets, at the cost of some temporal
          resolution. MicroNet automatically limits your selection so the model always retains at least 8
          time points for fitting. If an analysis still runs out of memory, MicroNet automatically retries
          it with progressively larger time step sizes before reporting a failure, so large datasets have
          the best chance of completing without manual intervention.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%= render "layouts/footer" %>
  </body>
</html>